 ## Overview
-This method computes the gradient of the rendered image with respect to scene parameters $\theta$ by integrating differential measurement contributions over path space [S1]. It solves the problem of high variance in gradient estimation for specular materials and complex lighting by combining standard BRDF sampling with Next Event Estimation (NEE) using Multiple Importance Sampling (MIS) [S1]. Unlike standard NEE which only connects paths to emitters at the final vertex, this method allows the differential vertex (the location where the derivative is taken) to be located at any intermediate vertex $i$ or at the emitter connection, combining BRDF and emitter sampling probabilities into a single mixture weight [S1]. It builds on [Reparameterization Estimators](6499-reparameterization-estimators.md) by applying the reparameterization principle to path-space integrals with explicit MIS weights for discontinuous contributions.
